        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1035">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1035: Disciplinary Cultures and the Diffusion of Artificial Intelligence: A Comparative Bibliometric Study of Music and Acoustics</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1035</link>
	<description>Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used in scientific research and artistic practice, yet its publication patterns vary across research contexts. This study presents a comparative bibliometric analysis of 655 publications retrieved from five databases. We examine publication trends, authorship patterns, national contributions, application areas, and the reported use of AI model families across three analytical categories: STEM&amp;amp;ndash;Sound-only (SSO), STEM&amp;amp;ndash;Music-explicit (SME), and Music-Focused AI (MFAI), where STEM denotes science, technology, engineering, and mathematics. The corpus comprises 139 SSO, 496 SME, and 20 MFAI publications. Publication output increased markedly after 2018, particularly in SSO and SME. Within the retrieved corpus, recurrent neural networks, convolutional neural networks, and Transformers appeared earlier in SME than in SSO, whereas MFAI included earlier work applying machine learning to sound design. Sole-authored papers accounted for 60% of MFAI publications, compared with 15.5% in SME and 2.2% in SSO. China had the largest publication count in SSO and SME, while no contributions from Chinese institutional affiliations were identified in the MFAI sample. These findings document differences in research output, collaboration patterns, geographical participation, and the timing of reported model use across publication contexts. They provide an empirical basis for further investigation of interdisciplinary exchange.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-17</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1035: Disciplinary Cultures and the Diffusion of Artificial Intelligence: A Comparative Bibliometric Study of Music and Acoustics</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1035">doi: 10.3390/info17101035</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Yintao Wang
		Xiaoqian Zhang
		Yue Wang
		</p>
	<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used in scientific research and artistic practice, yet its publication patterns vary across research contexts. This study presents a comparative bibliometric analysis of 655 publications retrieved from five databases. We examine publication trends, authorship patterns, national contributions, application areas, and the reported use of AI model families across three analytical categories: STEM&amp;amp;ndash;Sound-only (SSO), STEM&amp;amp;ndash;Music-explicit (SME), and Music-Focused AI (MFAI), where STEM denotes science, technology, engineering, and mathematics. The corpus comprises 139 SSO, 496 SME, and 20 MFAI publications. Publication output increased markedly after 2018, particularly in SSO and SME. Within the retrieved corpus, recurrent neural networks, convolutional neural networks, and Transformers appeared earlier in SME than in SSO, whereas MFAI included earlier work applying machine learning to sound design. Sole-authored papers accounted for 60% of MFAI publications, compared with 15.5% in SME and 2.2% in SSO. China had the largest publication count in SSO and SME, while no contributions from Chinese institutional affiliations were identified in the MFAI sample. These findings document differences in research output, collaboration patterns, geographical participation, and the timing of reported model use across publication contexts. They provide an empirical basis for further investigation of interdisciplinary exchange.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Disciplinary Cultures and the Diffusion of Artificial Intelligence: A Comparative Bibliometric Study of Music and Acoustics</dc:title>
			<dc:creator>Yintao Wang</dc:creator>
			<dc:creator>Xiaoqian Zhang</dc:creator>
			<dc:creator>Yue Wang</dc:creator>
