        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/989">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 989: An Improved Online Latent Dirichlet Allocation Model with Adaptive Topic Inheritance for Hot Event Mining and Evolution Tracking</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/989</link>
	<description>Aiming at hot event mining and evolutionary tracking over temporal text streams, this paper proposes an Improved Online Latent Dirichlet Allocation (IOLDA) model integrated with the topic inheritance mechanism to address the drawbacks of the original Online Latent Dirichlet Allocation (OLDA), including fixed historical weights, semantic confusion between emerging and legacy topics, and global vocabulary expansion. Based on the first-order Markov assumption, the IOLDA model takes the topic-word distribution of the previous time slice as the modeling prior and dynamically adjusts inheritance factors according to topic popularity to assign different inheritance strengths to historical semantics. Parameters are only transmitted over the intersection vocabulary of adjacent slices to reduce computational overhead induced by vocabulary expansion. This paper adopts symmetric Jensen&amp;amp;ndash;Shannon (JS) divergence to quantify semantic discrepancies between topics across adjacent slices and distinguishes three evolutionary states: topic persistence, extinction, and mutation. Subsequently, homologous topics are linked across time slices to construct event evolution chains. Same-index topic pairs are treated as candidate temporal links and retained only after JS-based semantic validation. On Reuters-21578, IOLDA reduces perplexity from 1039.6 to 1011.7 and increases coherence score from 0.454 to 0.476 relative to OLDA. Average alignment accuracy rises from 0.817 to 0.859, while controlled emerging topic F1 rises from 0.818 to 0.854 and delay falls from 0.88 to 0.56 slices. Efficiency results show lower overall time and memory use and higher throughput. Findings are limited to the datasets and setting and do not establish universal superiority over multi-window OLDA, explicit matching methods, or alternative new-word priors.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-07</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 989: An Improved Online Latent Dirichlet Allocation Model with Adaptive Topic Inheritance for Hot Event Mining and Evolution Tracking</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/989">doi: 10.3390/info17100989</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Wei Wang
		Hangyu Hu
		</p>
	<p>Aiming at hot event mining and evolutionary tracking over temporal text streams, this paper proposes an Improved Online Latent Dirichlet Allocation (IOLDA) model integrated with the topic inheritance mechanism to address the drawbacks of the original Online Latent Dirichlet Allocation (OLDA), including fixed historical weights, semantic confusion between emerging and legacy topics, and global vocabulary expansion. Based on the first-order Markov assumption, the IOLDA model takes the topic-word distribution of the previous time slice as the modeling prior and dynamically adjusts inheritance factors according to topic popularity to assign different inheritance strengths to historical semantics. Parameters are only transmitted over the intersection vocabulary of adjacent slices to reduce computational overhead induced by vocabulary expansion. This paper adopts symmetric Jensen&amp;amp;ndash;Shannon (JS) divergence to quantify semantic discrepancies between topics across adjacent slices and distinguishes three evolutionary states: topic persistence, extinction, and mutation. Subsequently, homologous topics are linked across time slices to construct event evolution chains. Same-index topic pairs are treated as candidate temporal links and retained only after JS-based semantic validation. On Reuters-21578, IOLDA reduces perplexity from 1039.6 to 1011.7 and increases coherence score from 0.454 to 0.476 relative to OLDA. Average alignment accuracy rises from 0.817 to 0.859, while controlled emerging topic F1 rises from 0.818 to 0.854 and delay falls from 0.88 to 0.56 slices. Efficiency results show lower overall time and memory use and higher throughput. Findings are limited to the datasets and setting and do not establish universal superiority over multi-window OLDA, explicit matching methods, or alternative new-word priors.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>An Improved Online Latent Dirichlet Allocation Model with Adaptive Topic Inheritance for Hot Event Mining and Evolution Tracking</dc:title>
