        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/976">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 976: A Pose Landmark-Based Lightweight Human Fall-Detection Framework Using Spatiotemporal Features</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/976</link>
	<description>In today&amp;amp;rsquo;s society, with its rapidly growing aging population, automated lightweight human fall detection has become very important. We present a lightweight human fall-detection framework based on pose landmarks and dynamic spatiotemporal motion features. The proposed system integrates YOLO12n and MediaPipe to detect human regions and extract pose landmarks from video sequences. The system incorporates a traceability information panel that visualizes the effects of different features on the classification of a frame as either a fall or an Activity of Daily Living (ADL) event. We introduce four override conditions, namely Head Position Override, Aspect Ratio Override, Vertical Leg Override, and Leg Alignment Override, to suppress false positives caused by ADL events such as bending, sleeping, picking up objects from the ground, and performing exercises. In addition, we utilize seven criteria, namely body angle, vertical drop, height ratio, angle change, knee-ankle vertical distance, vertical velocity, and head proximity to the ground, to distinguish fall events from ADL events effectively. We perform temporal validation of a fall event by confirming at least two consecutive fall frames to further reduce false positives. To evaluate the proposed method, we used two datasets, namely GMDCSA-24 and URFD. The proposed system achieved accuracy values of 90.63% and 88.57% on the GMDCSA-24 and URFD datasets, respectively.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-03</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 976: A Pose Landmark-Based Lightweight Human Fall-Detection Framework Using Spatiotemporal Features</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/976">doi: 10.3390/info17100976</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Ekram Alam
		Abu Sufian
		Paramartha Dutta
		Marco Leo
		</p>
	<p>In today&amp;amp;rsquo;s society, with its rapidly growing aging population, automated lightweight human fall detection has become very important. We present a lightweight human fall-detection framework based on pose landmarks and dynamic spatiotemporal motion features. The proposed system integrates YOLO12n and MediaPipe to detect human regions and extract pose landmarks from video sequences. The system incorporates a traceability information panel that visualizes the effects of different features on the classification of a frame as either a fall or an Activity of Daily Living (ADL) event. We introduce four override conditions, namely Head Position Override, Aspect Ratio Override, Vertical Leg Override, and Leg Alignment Override, to suppress false positives caused by ADL events such as bending, sleeping, picking up objects from the ground, and performing exercises. In addition, we utilize seven criteria, namely body angle, vertical drop, height ratio, angle change, knee-ankle vertical distance, vertical velocity, and head proximity to the ground, to distinguish fall events from ADL events effectively. We perform temporal validation of a fall event by confirming at least two consecutive fall frames to further reduce false positives. To evaluate the proposed method, we used two datasets, namely GMDCSA-24 and URFD. The proposed system achieved accuracy values of 90.63% and 88.57% on the GMDCSA-24 and URFD datasets, respectively.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>A Pose Landmark-Based Lightweight Human Fall-Detection Framework Using Spatiotemporal Features</dc:title>
